calculation suite."})]})]})]}),s.jsx("p",{className:"text-center text-[11px] text-slate-500 mt-6 font-bengali",children:"ঢাকা ডিএপি ২০২২-২০৩৫ ও ইমারত নির্মাণ বিধিমালা ২০২৫ অনুসারী"})]})]})},aTe=[{q:"What is the FAR for a 3-katha plot?",a:"There is no single figure for a 3-katha plot, because FAR depends on the plot's density block (Area FAR) and the width of the road in front of it (Road FAR), not on katha size alone. A 3-katha plot is 2,160 sq ft (about 200 sq m). For illustration, at a FAR of 3.0 the maximum floor area would be 2,160 × 3.0 = 6,480 sq ft. Enter your plot into the calculator to get the FAR that applies to it."},{q:"Does FAR change with road width?",a:"Yes. The Road FAR (Plot FAR) depends on the width of the road fronting your plot, and a wider road generally allows a higher Road FAR. Your approved FAR is the lower of the Road FAR and the DAP Area FAR, so road width can be the limiting factor on your plot."},{q:"How do I convert katha to square feet for a FAR calculation?",a:"One katha is 720 square feet (about 66.89 square metres). Multiply your plot size in katha by 720 to get the plot area in square feet, then multiply that by the FAR to get the maximum floor area."},{q:"Can a plot get more than its base FAR?",a:"In some cases. Incentives such as green-certified buildings, affordable flats, transit-oriented development (TOD), a new school or a new playground can add bonus FAR to the base FAR, but the total cannot exceed the maximum FAR cap for the building type, and some incentives apply only to certain building types. The calculator shows which incentives apply to your project."}],iTe="FAR = Total Floor Area ÷ Plot Area",oTe=[[s.jsx(T0,{}),"Mark Your Plot","Locate your plot and inspect available DAP zone and location-specific information."],[s.jsx(pP,{}),"Plot Size & Road Width","Review FAR, floor-area distribution, plot size, and road width — not just one final number."],[s.jsx(jl,{}),"Check Incentives","Review applicable control, affordable fla